### Structures in C Programming

A **structure** (`struct`) in C is a user-defined composite data type that enables grouping together related variables of different data types under a single unified name. While an array stores multiple elements of the *same* data type, a structure allows heterogeneous collection (integers, floats, characters, arrays, pointers).

#### Why Structures are Used:
1. **Data Organization:** Packages real-world entities (e.g., student, book, bank account) into a coherent object.
2. **Modularity & Readability:** Functions can accept and return entire structured entities rather than long lists of disparate parameters.
3. **Contiguous Allocation:** All members of a structure are laid out contiguously in memory (subject to alignment padding).

#### Code Example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// 1. Defining a structure
struct Student {
    int rollNo;
    char name[50];
    float cgpa;
};

int main() {
    // 2. Initializing structures
    // Method A: Direct compile-time initialization list
    struct Student s1 = {101, "Aditi Sharma", 9.4};

    // Method B: Member-by-member assignment
    struct Student s2;
    s2.rollNo = 102;
    strcpy(s2.name, "Varun Kumar");
    s2.cgpa = 8.9;

    // 3. Accessing structure members using the dot (.) operator
    printf("Student 1: Roll No: %d | Name: %s | CGPA: %.2f\n", 
           s1.rollNo, s1.name, s1.cgpa);
    printf("Student 2: Roll No: %d | Name: %s | CGPA: %.2f\n", 
           s2.rollNo, s2.name, s2.cgpa);

    return 0;
}
```

### Dynamic Order Management System

#### a) Structure Definition (Pseudocode & C):

```text
Structure OrderNode:
    orderId: Integer
    next: Pointer to OrderNode
End Structure

```
```c
struct OrderNode {
    int orderId;
    struct OrderNode* next;
};

```

#### b) Insertion Functions:

**1. Insert at the Beginning (Prepending):**

```text
Function InsertAtBeginning(Head, newId) -> Pointer to OrderNode:
    newNode = Allocate OrderNode
    newNode.orderId = newId
    newNode.next = Head
    Head = newNode
    Return Head
End Function

```
*Time Complexity:* $O(1)$.

**2. Insert at the End (Appending):**

```text
Function InsertAtEnd(Head, newId) -> Pointer to OrderNode:
    newNode = Allocate OrderNode
    newNode.orderId = newId
    newNode.next = NULL

    If Head == NULL then
        Return newNode
    End If

    curr = Head
    While curr.next != NULL do
        curr = curr.next
    End While
    curr.next = newNode

    Return Head
End Function

```
*Time Complexity:* $O(n)$ ($O(1)$ if tail pointer is maintained).

#### C Implementation:

```c
struct OrderNode* insertAtBeginning(struct OrderNode* head, int orderId) {
    struct OrderNode* newNode = (struct OrderNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct OrderNode));
    newNode->orderId = orderId;
    newNode->next = head;
    return newNode;
}

struct OrderNode* insertAtEnd(struct OrderNode* head, int orderId) {
    struct OrderNode* newNode = (struct OrderNode*)malloc(sizeof(struct OrderNode));
    newNode->orderId = orderId;
    newNode->next = NULL;
    if (head == NULL) return newNode;
    struct OrderNode* temp = head;
    while (temp->next != NULL) temp = temp->next;
    temp->next = newNode;
    return head;
}
```

### Josephus Problem Implementation using Circular Linked List

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

struct Person {
    int id;
    struct Person* next;
};

// Function to simulate the Josephus Game
int josephusGame(int n, int k) {
    if (n <= 0 || k <= 0) return -1;

    // 1. Build circular linked list of n players
    struct Person* head = (struct Person*)malloc(sizeof(struct Person));
    head->id = 1;
    struct Person* prev = head;

    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        struct Person* p = (struct Person*)malloc(sizeof(struct Person));
        p->id = i;
        prev->next = p;
        prev = p;
    }
    prev->next = head; // Close circle

    printf("Starting Josephus Game with %d people, eliminating every %d-th person:\n", n, k);

    // 2. Elimination round until only 1 person remains
    struct Person* curr = head;
    while (curr->next != curr) {
        // Count k-1 steps
        for (int count = 1; count < k - 1; count++) {
            curr = curr->next;
        }

        // curr->next is the k-th person to be eliminated
        struct Person* eliminated = curr->next;
        printf("Person %d is eliminated.\n", eliminated->id);
        curr->next = eliminated->next;
        free(eliminated);

        // Move to the next person
        curr = curr->next;
    }

    int survivorId = curr->id;
    free(curr);
    return survivorId;
}

int main() {
    int n = 7, k = 3;
    int winner = josephusGame(n, k);
    printf("\nThe last surviving person is: %d\n", winner);
    return 0;
}

```

**Output:**

```text
Starting Josephus Game with 7 people, eliminating every 3-th person:
Person 3 is eliminated.
Person 6 is eliminated.
Person 2 is eliminated.
Person 7 is eliminated.
Person 5 is eliminated.
Person 1 is eliminated.

The last surviving person is: 4
```

### Contact List Application Using Self-Referential Structure

#### A) Definition of Self-Referential Structure:
A self-referential structure contains a pointer member that points to another structure of the exact same type.

```c
struct Contact {
    char name[50];
    char phone[15];
    struct Contact* next; // Self-referential pointer
};

```

#### B) Adding a New Contact (Insertion):
**Steps to link after an existing contact:**
1. Allocate dynamic memory for `newContact` on the heap using `malloc(sizeof(struct Contact))`.
2. Populate `newContact->name` and `newContact->phone`.
3. Link the new contact: point `newContact->next` to the node that currently follows the target existing contact (`existingContact->next`).
4. Update the existing contact's pointer: `existingContact->next = newContact`.

```c
void addContactAfter(struct Contact* existingContact, const char* name, const char* phone) {
    if (existingContact == NULL) return;
    struct Contact* newContact = (struct Contact*)malloc(sizeof(struct Contact));
    strcpy(newContact->name, name);
    strcpy(newContact->phone, phone);
    newContact->next = existingContact->next;
    existingContact->next = newContact;
}

```

#### C) Displaying Contacts (Traversal):
**Traversal Logic:**
1. Initialize a temporary traversal pointer `curr` to the starting contact.
2. Use a `while` loop with condition `curr != NULL`.
3. In each iteration, print `curr->name` and `curr->phone`.
4. Advance `curr` to the next linked contact: `curr = curr->next`.

```c
void displayContacts(struct Contact* start) {
    struct Contact* curr = start;
    printf("\n--- Contact List Traversal ---\n");
    while (curr != NULL) {
        printf("Name: %-20s | Phone: %s\n", curr->name, curr->phone);
        curr = curr->next;
    }
}

```

#### D) Deleting a Specific Contact:
**Steps for Deletion:**
1. If the contact to be deleted is the head, update `head = head->next`, then `free(oldHead)`.
2. Otherwise, traverse the list maintaining a `prev` pointer until `curr` points to the contact matching the target name/phone.
3. Relink to bypass the target node: `prev->next = curr->next`.
4. Deallocate memory of `curr` using `free(curr)` to prevent memory leaks.

```c
struct Contact* deleteContact(struct Contact* head, const char* targetName) {
    if (head == NULL) return NULL;
    if (strcmp(head->name, targetName) == 0) {
        struct Contact* temp = head;
        head = head->next;
        free(temp);
        return head;
    }
    struct Contact *curr = head, *prev = NULL;
    while (curr != NULL && strcmp(curr->name, targetName) != 0) {
        prev = curr;
        curr = curr->next;
    }
    if (curr != NULL) {
        prev->next = curr->next;
        free(curr);
    }
    return head;
}
```

### Complete C Program for Dynamic Matrix Multiplication

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// A) Allocate Memory Dynamically
int** allocateMatrix(int rows, int cols) {
    int **mat = (int **)malloc(rows * sizeof(int *));
    if (mat == NULL) exit(1);
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        mat[i] = (int *)malloc(cols * sizeof(int));
        if (mat[i] == NULL) exit(1);
    }
    return mat;
}

// B) Input Matrix Values
void inputMatrix(int **mat, int rows, int cols, const char *name) {
    printf("Enter values for Matrix %s (%dx%d):\n", name, rows, cols);
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &mat[i][j]);
        }
    }
}

// C) Perform Matrix Multiplication: C = A x B
int** multiplyMatrices(int **A, int r1, int c1, int **B, int r2, int c2) {
    int **C = allocateMatrix(r1, c2);
    for (int i = 0; i < r1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < c2; j++) {
            C[i][j] = 0;
            for (int k = 0; k < c1; k++) {
                C[i][j] += A[i][k] * B[k][j];
            }
        }
    }
    return C;
}

// D) Display Matrix
void displayMatrix(int **mat, int rows, int cols) {
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < cols; j++) {
            printf("%5d ", mat[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
}

// E) Deallocate Memory
void freeMatrix(int **mat, int rows) {
    if (mat == NULL) return;
    for (int i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
        free(mat[i]);
    }
    free(mat);
}

int main() {
    int r1, c1, r2, c2;
    printf("Enter dimensions for Matrix A (rows cols): ");
    scanf("%d %d", &r1, &c1);
    printf("Enter dimensions for Matrix B (rows cols): ");
    scanf("%d %d", &r2, &c2);

    if (c1 != r2) {
        printf("Matrix multiplication error: Columns of A (%d) must equal Rows of B (%d).\n", c1, r2);
        return 1;
    }

    // A & B: Allocate and Input
    int **A = allocateMatrix(r1, c1);
    int **B = allocateMatrix(r2, c2);
    inputMatrix(A, r1, c1, "A");
    inputMatrix(B, r2, c2, "B");

    // C: Multiply
    int **C = multiplyMatrices(A, r1, c1, B, r2, c2);

    // D: Display
    printf("\nResultant Matrix C (A x B):\n");
    displayMatrix(C, r1, c2);

    // E: Deallocate Memory
    freeMatrix(A, r1);
    freeMatrix(B, r2);
    freeMatrix(C, r1);
    printf("\nMemory successfully deallocated.\n");

    return 0;
}
```

### Array-Based Inventory Management System

#### 1. Data Structure Design:
We use a fixed-capacity contiguous array of `struct Product`. An integer `size` tracks the current number of active products.

```c
#define MAX_PRODUCTS 100

struct Product {
    int productId;
    char name[50];
    float price;
    int quantity;
};

struct Product inventory[MAX_PRODUCTS];
int productCount = 0;

```

#### 2. Operation Descriptions & Algorithms:

**A. Insertion (Maintaining Sorted Order by `productId`):**
1. Check if array is full (`productCount == MAX_PRODUCTS`). If so, throw overflow error.
2. Find the index $i$ where the new product should be placed using linear scan or binary search such that $\text{inventory}[i].\text{productId} > \text{newId}$.
3. Shift all elements from index $\text{productCount} - 1$ down to $i$ one position to the right.
4. Place the new product at index $i$ and increment $\text{productCount}$.
- *Complexity:* $O(n)$ time due to shifting elements.

**B. Deletion (By `productId`):**
1. Search for the product with the target `productId`. Since the array is maintained in sorted order, we can use **Binary Search** ($O(\log n)$) to locate its index $k$.
2. If found, shift all elements from index $k + 1$ to $\text{productCount} - 1$ one position to the left.
3. Decrement $\text{productCount}$.
- *Complexity:* $O(n)$ time due to left shifting.

**C. Traversal:**
1. Iterate directly through indices $0$ to $\text{productCount} - 1$.
2. Since elements are maintained in sorted order, traversal naturally outputs the inventory sorted by Product ID.
- *Complexity:* $O(n)$ time.

#### 3. C Implementation:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

#define MAX 100

struct Product {
    int id;
    char name[30];
    float price;
};

struct Product inventory[MAX];
int count = 0;

void insertProduct(int id, const char* name, float price) {
    if (count >= MAX) { printf("Inventory Full!\n"); return; }
    int i = count - 1;
    while (i >= 0 && inventory[i].id > id) {
        inventory[i + 1] = inventory[i]; // Shift right
        i--;
    }
    inventory[i + 1].id = id;
    strcpy(inventory[i + 1].name, name);
    inventory[i + 1].price = price;
    count++;
    printf("Product %d inserted successfully.\n", id);
}

void deleteProduct(int id) {
    int pos = -1;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        if (inventory[i].id == id) { pos = i; break; }
    }
    if (pos == -1) { printf("Product %d not found!\n", id); return; }
    for (int i = pos; i < count - 1; i++) {
        inventory[i] = inventory[i + 1]; // Shift left
    }
    count--;
    printf("Product %d deleted successfully.\n", id);
}

void displayInventory() {
    printf("\n--- Current Inventory List (Sorted by ID) ---\n");
    printf("%-8s %-20s %-10s\n", "ID", "Name", "Price");
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        printf("%-8d %-20s $%-9.2f\n", inventory[i].id, inventory[i].name, inventory[i].price);
    }
}
```
